IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `miles-card-domain`
IMPORT `miles-card`

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE HOUSEHOLD CHEAT SHEET, RESTATED AS CLAIMS
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE IS. Every `#ASSERT` below is a claim made by a SECONDARY
-- SOURCE: "Miles Card Stack -- Routing Cheat Sheet", updated June 2026, a
-- private household routing note written from blog posts, bank marketing
-- pages and the household's own statements. It is NOT an issuer's terms and
-- conditions. It is deposited in this corpus as the additional encoding
-- `cheatsheet-2026-06`, alongside the encodings taken from the T&Cs
-- themselves, so that the two can be compared.
--
-- WRITTEN BLIND. Whoever wrote this file read three things: the cheat sheet,
-- `miles-card-domain.l4` (types only), and the signature of
-- `the rate on` in `miles-card.l4`. No issuer T&C, no extract of one, and no
-- card rule module was opened. That is the point: an acceptance test written
-- from the rules it tests is not a test, it is a transcription of them.
--
-- FAILURES ARE EXPECTED, AND ARE THE DELIVERABLE. A cheat sheet is a
-- compression. It rounds ("~S$822"), it merges conditions the T&C keeps
-- separate, it states rates that depend on promotions whose terms this corpus
-- does not hold (the domain module says so of the Solitaire savings uplift),
-- and it is nine months old in a market that reprices quarterly. Every
-- assertion that fails is one of four things, and telling them apart is the
-- work this file exists to make possible:
--     (a) the cheat sheet is wrong, and the household is routing spend badly;
--     (b) the cheat sheet is right and the rule module has a defect;
--     (c) both are defensible and the T&C is genuinely ambiguous;
--     (d) the T&C that would settle it is not held, so the rule answers
--         Unconfirmed while the cheat sheet answers with a number.
-- Nothing here should be "fixed" by editing this file to agree with the
-- rules. Editing a claim to match the answer destroys the only evidence the
-- comparison produces.
--
-- HOW MANY CLAIMS. 184 assertions as this line is written. The figure is
-- here because it was asked for, but do not trust it against the file: the
-- file is the authority, and the command is `grep -c` on `^#ASSERT`. A count
-- in prose is the first thing to go stale when someone adds a row.
--
-- DO NOT CORRECT THE SOURCE. These assertions transcribe the sheet's cells,
-- including where the transcriber believed a cell to be wrong. The cheat
-- sheet's own figure is what is asserted, always.
--
-- WHAT IS SKIPPED, AND WHY.
--   * SC Smart is a CASHBACK card: "6% cashback", "~S$60/mo rebate". It has
--     no rate in miles, and `Rate answer` answers in miles per dollar, so
--     there is nothing to assert. One waterfall row -- Streaming
--     (Netflix/Disney+/HBO/Spotify) -- names SC Smart and nothing else, and
--     is skipped whole. Three further rows (Fast food, Public transport, and
--     Guardian's SC overlap note) name SC Smart beside miles cards; there the
--     miles cards are asserted and the SC Smart cell is passed over.
--   * DBS Live Fresh is listed in the stack as "cashback, outside the miles
--     stack", is in no waterfall row, and is not in `Card`. Nothing to assert.
--
-- WHERE THIS FILE SITS, AND WHY NOT IN A SUBDIRECTORY. It was to live at
-- `cheatsheet/cheatsheet-claims.l4`. L4's import resolution has no
-- parent-directory step -- measured: the candidate list it prints for a file
-- in `cheatsheet/` is project root, the importer's OWN directory,
-- JL4_LIBRARY_PATH, XDG and the bundle, and never `../`. So a file one level
-- down cannot reach `miles-card.l4` at all. It sits beside the module it
-- tests instead.
--
-- HOW A PURCHASE IS ENCODED. The cheat sheet tells you how to pay as well as
-- what to pay with, and the domain separates the two axes the phrase
-- "contactless" runs together: the CREDENTIAL the cardholder presents, and
-- the INDICATOR the merchant's acquirer attaches. Only the first of those is
-- a fixture fact. The transcription rule, applied uniformly and recorded
-- here so a reader can audit it in one place:
--     "contactless" / "Apple Pay default"  -> tapped + wallet token
--     "online" / "if ordered online" / "pay in app" / "in-app billing"
--                                          -> website-or-app + physical card
--     row states no channel, purchase is in person
--                                          -> tapped + physical card
--     the merchant indicator, on every row -> Unknown
--
-- WHY THE INDICATOR IS ALWAYS UNKNOWN -- revised 2026-09-21, and the reason
-- is worth the paragraph because an earlier draft of this file did it the
-- other way. A fixture may carry only facts a witness could depose to AT THE
-- MOMENT OF PAYING. The acquirer's system indicator is not one: the domain
-- module quotes three issuers saying in terms that they do not determine it
-- and the merchant's banker does, and it is attached when the charge is
-- posted, after the cardholder has acted. A transcription that filled it in
-- from the row's wording -- "online" cells flagged online, "contactless"
-- cells flagged card-present -- was quietly deciding the very fact the
-- domain built an explicit `Unknown` for, and every such fixture asserted a
-- rate against a world the cardholder cannot observe. So `Unknown` is
-- uniform here. No row is special-cased, the two grocery rows that say "if
-- ordered online" included, and the one cell that claims a wallet tap
-- "counts as online" included: that cell is a claim about the OUTCOME, and
-- the fixture records only the ACT.
--
-- WHAT THAT COSTS, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISREAD AS A DEFECT. The domain says
-- `miles per dollar` carries the LOWER of the two conditional branches when
-- the indicator is Unknown, so that a headline figure never overstates. The
-- cheat sheet's cells are written as though the indicator were settled. The
-- two will therefore disagree on any row whose rate turns on the indicator,
-- and that disagreement is category (d) above -- not the sheet being wrong
-- about a rate, and not the rule module having a defect. `Rate answer` also
-- carries `miles per dollar if the merchant flags online` and its
-- card-present twin; asserting the sheet's figures against THOSE would be a
-- different and also interesting comparison, and is deliberately not what
-- this file does. It asserts the headline.
--
-- WHY EVERY NAME HERE STARTS "S5". Every name this file defines -- the
-- cardholder, the month, the purchase constructors and all 32 purchases --
-- carries the `S5 ` prefix this file already uses to mark the secondary
-- source. It is not decoration. The card rule modules define their own
-- illustration fixtures, they are in scope here through the composer, and
-- they use the obvious names. Measured 2026-09-21: a fixture called
-- `foodpanda` collided with a `Merchant` constructor AND with a
-- `Participating Merchant` constructor, three definitions of one token
-- resolved by type at each use site; renaming it to `a foodpanda order`
-- collided with a fixture of that exact name and the SAME type, Transaction,
-- in a card module -- which type cannot disambiguate, so it is a hard error.
-- A prefix that names the source fixes the whole class instead of the two
-- instances found, and those modules are being written right now, so a
-- name that is free at this moment is not evidence it will stay free.
--
-- ONE MONTH, ONE CARDHOLDER. The sheet's rates are the rates AFTER the
-- monthly unlocks are hit -- its first section is headed "Must hit every
-- month". So the fixture month is one in which the yuu S$800 / 4-merchant
-- threshold IS met and the PAssion thresholds ARE met, and the cardholder has
-- Dining and Family elected on Solitaire with yuu and PAssion linked. Cap
-- ROOM is not modelled (the domain says allocation is out of scope), so each
-- row is asserted as though the card named still had room.
-- ===========================================================================


§ `The fixtures`

§§ `The cardholder`

-- Stack table: Solitaire "Dining out, supermarkets, kids' stores" with
-- "S$750 Dining + S$750 Family" -> Dining and Family are the two elected
-- categories. yuu: "pay with yuu card and scan yuu app" -> linked. PAssion:
-- "yuu app linked" -> linked. The HSBC Everyday Global Account is not
-- mentioned anywhere on the cheat sheet, so its ADB is 0.
`S5 the cardholder` MEANS
    Cardholder WITH
        `Solitaire preferred rewards categories`              IS LIST Dining, Family
        `yuu account linked to the card`                      IS TRUE
        `HSBC Everyday Global Account deposits ADB this month` IS 0
        `PAssion membership registered and card linked in yuu` IS TRUE

§§ `The month`

-- "yuu -- S$800 across 4 different merchants" and PAssion's "~S$500 + 5
-- categories/mo to unlock", split across the two spend figures the domain
-- carries. The sheet's rates all assume these are met.
`S5 the month` MEANS
    `Month position` WITH
        `yuu eligible spend so far this calendar month`                     IS 800
        `yuu participating merchants transacted so far this calendar month` IS 4
        `PAssion spend at Participating Stores so far this month`           IS 300
        `PAssion spend at non-Participating Stores so far this month`       IS 400

§§ `How a purchase is built`

-- Four constructors, one per way the cheat sheet tells you to pay, and a
-- fifth that re-pays a purchase the file already defines. Named fields
-- throughout: positional `OF` would be shorter but three of the fifteen
-- fields are STRINGs and a transposition among them is silent.
--
-- `merchant indicator` is `Unknown` in every one of them, with no exception
-- and no per-row override. The header says why at length.
--
-- FOUR FIELDS THE DOMAIN ADDED ON 2026-09-21, and what a secondary source
-- can honestly put in them. (Four, not the three I was told to expect: the
-- module gained `posted in a foreign currency` while this file was being
-- updated, and the compiler found it. Anything a later reader takes from
-- this paragraph should be checked against the module, not against me.) `merchant` is a closed enumeration of the
-- merchants some HELD T&C names, and it is passed in per fixture rather than
-- derived from the printed name -- a transcription, never a computation from
-- a string. Where the cheat sheet's row names a merchant the enumeration
-- holds, that constructor is used; where it does not, `Another merchant`,
-- which is the honest answer and not a fallback: the enumeration is a list
-- of merchants the ISSUERS name, and most of the household's spend is not
-- at one.
--
-- `description patterns` is EMPTY on every fixture. The cheat sheet says
-- nothing whatever about statement lines -- it never quotes one -- so there
-- is no pattern to transcribe, and inventing one would be this file
-- asserting a fact its source does not contain. EMPTY here means "the source
-- is silent", not "the statement line matched nothing", and a rule that
-- turns on an exclusion pattern will therefore answer as though none applied.
--
-- `charge kind` is `Retail purchase` on every fixture. Every row of the
-- waterfall is a purchase of goods or services; the sheet routes no fee, no
-- instalment, no cash advance and no refund, and its one bill-payment row
-- (StarHub) it explicitly routes "via app/site, not AXS", which is a
-- purchase-shaped charge and not internet banking.
--
-- `posted in a foreign currency` is a boolean the domain keeps separate from
-- the `currency` string, because a rule may not compare strings. It is TRUE
-- on exactly the two purchases the sheet itself calls foreign: the Malaysian
-- Ringgit row, and the overseas half of "Everything else / overseas". It is
-- FALSE everywhere else, which is what a sheet headed "KrisFlyer-focused"
-- and priced in S$ throughout is saying. Note this is a THIRD axis, beside
-- `currency` and `made in Singapore`, and the fixtures set all three
-- consistently rather than deriving any one from another.
--
-- WHAT THE SHEET DOES NOT SAY HERE, recorded because it was nearly written
-- in. The sheet contains no statement about the currency a charge is BILLED
-- in -- no dynamic-currency-conversion language, nothing about paying in the
-- foreign currency rather than in converted S$. Searched 2026-09-21: the
-- strings "never converted", "converted to SGD" and "pay in the foreign" do
-- not occur. Its whole foreign-currency vocabulary is four things -- "MYR
-- online", "o/s", "1.4 local / 2.4 overseas", "~1.2 local / ~2.2 overseas"
-- -- plus two FX-fee notes, "3.25% FX fee: local use only" on Solitaire and
-- "3.25% FX, local-only" on Revolution. So TRUE here transcribes only that
-- the sheet calls the row foreign. Anyone tempted to read a billing-currency
-- claim into this field should get it from a T&C, not from here.
--
-- Those two FX notes did constrain the transcription, and the constraint is
-- honoured: the sheet tells the household not to take Solitaire or
-- Revolution abroad, and accordingly neither foreign fixture asserts
-- anything about either card. Row 23 asserts PAssion and PRVI; row 24
-- asserts PRVI and PremierMiles.

GIVEN name IS A STRING, `the merchant` IS A Merchant, mcc IS A NUMBER, spend IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Transaction
`S5 a contactless tap at` name `the merchant` mcc spend MEANS
    Transaction WITH
        `merchant name`           IS name
        `merchant`                IS `the merchant`
        `merchant category code`  IS mcc
        `transaction description` IS name
        `description patterns`    IS EMPTY
        `charge kind`             IS `Retail purchase`
        `amount`                  IS spend
        `currency`                IS "SGD"
        `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
        `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 6 10
        `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 6 12
        `payment channel`         IS `Tapped at a terminal`
        `credential`              IS `A mobile wallet token`
        `merchant indicator`      IS Unknown
        `made in Singapore`       IS TRUE

GIVEN name IS A STRING, `the merchant` IS A Merchant, mcc IS A NUMBER, spend IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Transaction
`S5 a card tap at` name `the merchant` mcc spend MEANS
    Transaction WITH
        `merchant name`           IS name
        `merchant`                IS `the merchant`
        `merchant category code`  IS mcc
        `transaction description` IS name
        `description patterns`    IS EMPTY
        `charge kind`             IS `Retail purchase`
        `amount`                  IS spend
        `currency`                IS "SGD"
        `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
        `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 6 10
        `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 6 12
        `payment channel`         IS `Tapped at a terminal`
        `credential`              IS `The physical card`
        `merchant indicator`      IS Unknown
        `made in Singapore`       IS TRUE

GIVEN name IS A STRING, `the merchant` IS A Merchant, mcc IS A NUMBER, spend IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Transaction
`S5 an online purchase at` name `the merchant` mcc spend MEANS
    Transaction WITH
        `merchant name`           IS name
        `merchant`                IS `the merchant`
        `merchant category code`  IS mcc
        `transaction description` IS name
        `description patterns`    IS EMPTY
        `charge kind`             IS `Retail purchase`
        `amount`                  IS spend
        `currency`                IS "SGD"
        `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
        `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 6 10
        `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 6 12
        `payment channel`         IS `Paid in a website or app`
        `credential`              IS `The physical card`
        `merchant indicator`      IS Unknown
        `made in Singapore`       IS TRUE

GIVEN name IS A STRING, `the merchant` IS A Merchant, mcc IS A NUMBER, spend IS A NUMBER, ccy IS A STRING
GIVETH A Transaction
`S5 a foreign online purchase at` name `the merchant` mcc spend ccy MEANS
    Transaction WITH
        `merchant name`           IS name
        `merchant`                IS `the merchant`
        `merchant category code`  IS mcc
        `transaction description` IS name
        `description patterns`    IS EMPTY
        `charge kind`             IS `Retail purchase`
        `amount`                  IS spend
        `currency`                IS ccy
        `posted in a foreign currency` IS TRUE
        `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 6 10
        `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 6 12
        `payment channel`         IS `Paid in a website or app`
        `credential`              IS `The physical card`
        `merchant indicator`      IS Unknown
        `made in Singapore`       IS FALSE

-- "never via Apple/Google Pay" is a claim about the CREDENTIAL and about
-- nothing else, so it is tested by re-paying purchases this file already
-- defines rather than by inventing merchants for it. Every other field, the
-- payment channel included, carries over unchanged -- an online order
-- re-paid with a wallet stays an online order, at the same merchant.
--
-- L4 has no record-update operator: `someValue WITH field IS v` parses as
-- function application and errors ("you are giving named inputs to ... but
-- it is not a function"). So the fields are copied one at a time, each named
-- on both sides of its `IS`, which is what makes a transposition impossible
-- to write here.
GIVEN txn IS A Transaction
GIVETH A Transaction
`S5 the same purchase paid with a wallet token` txn MEANS
    Transaction WITH
        `merchant name`           IS txn's `merchant name`
        `merchant`                IS txn's `merchant`
        `merchant category code`  IS txn's `merchant category code`
        `transaction description` IS txn's `transaction description`
        `description patterns`    IS txn's `description patterns`
        `charge kind`             IS txn's `charge kind`
        `amount`                  IS txn's `amount`
        `currency`                IS txn's `currency`
        `posted in a foreign currency` IS txn's `posted in a foreign currency`
        `transaction date`        IS txn's `transaction date`
        `posting date`            IS txn's `posting date`
        `payment channel`         IS txn's `payment channel`
        `credential`              IS `A mobile wallet token`
        `merchant indicator`      IS txn's `merchant indicator`
        `made in Singapore`       IS txn's `made in Singapore`


§ `The spend-to-card waterfall`

-- Twenty-four rows, in the order the sheet prints them. Each row's comment
-- quotes the cell being restated, prefixed [S5] for the secondary source.
-- A row that names a card but prints no dollar figure gets a rate assertion
-- only; "uncap" is asserted as `capped` FALSE.


§§ `Row 1 -- Dining out`

`S5 dining out` MEANS `S5 a contactless tap at` "Cedele Restaurant Raffles City" `Another merchant` 5812 60

-- [S5] "Dining out (restaurants, MCC 5811/5812) | Lady's Solitaire -- Dining (6, S$750)"
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `miles per dollar` EQUALS 6
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `cap`'s `bonus spend per month` EQUALS 750
-- [S5] "2nd (1st cap full) | Revolution (4, S$1k, contactless)"
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `HSBC Revolution` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `miles per dollar` EQUALS 4
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `HSBC Revolution` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `cap`'s `bonus spend per month` EQUALS 1000
-- [S5] "3rd / overflow | PRVI (1.4, uncap)"
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `UOB PRVI Miles` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `miles per dollar` EQUALS 1.4
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `UOB PRVI Miles` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `cap`'s `capped` EQUALS FALSE


