#ASSERT (`the rate on` `Citi Rewards` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 lazada`)'s `cap`'s `attribution` EQUALS `By posting date`
-- [S5] stack table: "Citi Rewards | 4 mpd | S$1,000 online"
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `Citi Rewards` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 lazada`)'s `cap`'s `capped` EQUALS TRUE


§§ `HSBC Revolution -- one combined S$1,000`

-- [S5] stack table: "HSBC Revolution | 4 mpd | S$1,000 online + contactless (combined)"
-- The sheet's claim is that ONE figure covers both channels, so the same
-- S$1,000 is asserted against an online purchase and against a tap.
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `HSBC Revolution` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 lazada`)'s `cap`'s `bonus spend per month` EQUALS 1000
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `HSBC Revolution` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 a contactless tap`)'s `cap`'s `bonus spend per month` EQUALS 1000
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `HSBC Revolution` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 a contactless tap`)'s `cap`'s `capped` EQUALS TRUE


§§ `POSB PAssion Debit -- its own cap`

-- [S5] stack table: "PAssion POSB Debit | 5 mpd | ~S$507 yuu spend (own cap)"
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `POSB PAssion Debit` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 cold storage`)'s `cap`'s `capped` EQUALS TRUE


§§ `Which pot -- asserted by comparison, so no string is named`

-- The cap assertions above are about SIZE. These four are the sheet's claims
-- about pot IDENTITY, and each is written as a comparison between two
-- answers of the SAME card. No `pool` string appears, so nothing here can
-- fail on spelling: whatever the rule modules decide to call a pot, the
-- Dining pot either is or is not the Family pot. That is why these four are
-- safe for a secondary source to assert while the strings are still moving.

-- [S5] "Solitaire caps are per CATEGORY: S$750 Dining + S$750 Family = S$1,500 combined."
-- Read forwards, "per category" says each category is ONE pot: two dining
-- purchases share a pot, and two Family purchases share a pot.
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `cap`'s `pool` EQUALS (`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 a cafe`)'s `cap`'s `pool`
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 cold storage`)'s `cap`'s `pool` EQUALS (`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 kids wear in store`)'s `cap`'s `pool`
-- [S5] "Unused room in one category does NOT transfer to the other."
-- Read backwards, it says the two pots are not the same pot. Both halves are
-- needed: one pot per category would satisfy the first two assertions and
-- still be a single shared S$750.
#ASSERT NOT ((`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 dining out`)'s `cap`'s `pool` EQUALS (`the rate on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 cold storage`)'s `cap`'s `pool`)
-- [S5] stack table: "HSBC Revolution | 4 mpd | S$1,000 online + contactless (combined)"
-- The mirror image: two channels the sheet says share one pot.
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `HSBC Revolution` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 lazada`)'s `cap`'s `pool` EQUALS (`the rate on` `HSBC Revolution` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 a contactless tap`)'s `cap`'s `pool`


§ `Claims that turn on the credential itself`

-- Three cells make a claim about HOW you pay rather than about what you buy.
-- Each is encoded exactly as the cell words it, and none of them touches the
-- merchant indicator, which stays Unknown throughout.

§§ `Woman's World -- a wallet tap in store counts as online`

-- A tap, with a wallet token, at a department store. The cell's words are
-- that this COUNTS AS online; whether the acquirer flags it so is the
-- acquirer's business and is not a fact of the fixture, so the indicator is
-- Unknown here as everywhere. The claim being asserted is the cell's figure.
`S5 a wallet tap in a department store` MEANS `S5 a contactless tap at` "Takashimaya" `Another merchant` 5311 140

-- [S5] merchant reference: "*Earns:* any transaction processed as **online** (any MCC), ... and Kris+/mobile-wallet in-store (counts as online)"
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `DBS Woman's World` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 a wallet tap in a department store`)'s `miles per dollar` EQUALS 4
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `DBS Woman's World` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 a wallet tap in a department store`)'s `cap`'s `bonus spend per month` EQUALS 1000

§§ `Woman's World -- a physical tap does not`

-- [S5] merchant reference: "Physical contactless tap (non-wallet) also doesn't earn."
-- `S5 kids wear in store` is a tap with the physical card, at an MCC the sheet
-- otherwise routes to Woman's World when ordered online.
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `DBS Woman's World` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` `S5 kids wear in store`)'s `earns the bonus rate` EQUALS FALSE

§§ `Citi Rewards -- never via Apple or Google Pay`

-- [S5] stack table: "Citi Rewards | ... | have · never via Apple/Google Pay"
-- [S5] merchant reference: "Does NOT earn: **mobile wallet (Apple/Google Pay) -- pay by card or card-on-file**"
--
-- "NEVER" IS UNIVERSAL, SO IT IS INSTANTIATED UNIVERSALLY. A single witness
-- would test the claim at one merchant, one MCC and one channel, and pass
-- while the rule excluded wallets in exactly that one place. The cell
-- quantifies over all spend, so the assertion quantifies over every purchase
-- this file already defines -- 32 of them, each re-paid with a wallet
-- token and otherwise untouched. No merchant is invented for this.
--
-- Two notes for whoever reads a failure here. First, the sheet states no
-- base figure for Citi Rewards anywhere -- the only base rate it prints is
-- Revolution's "0.4 only" -- so the claim is asserted as the sheet makes it,
-- that the bonus rate is not earned, and no miles-per-dollar number is
-- invented for it. Second, this population includes purchases at merchants
-- and codes the sheet never routes to Citi Rewards at all. That is
-- deliberate and is not a transcription error: a card that does not earn its
-- bonus at a petrol-coded merchant anyway will satisfy the assertion for a
-- reason that has nothing to do with the wallet, so those rows are weak
-- evidence, and the rows where the sheet DOES route to Citi -- Lazada,
-- Shopee, Amazon, the StarHub bill, foodpanda -- are the load-bearing ones.
-- Listing all of them is still right: it is what "never" says, and a later
-- reader can see which subset carried the weight without re-deriving it.
--
-- For a purchase already paid with a wallet token -- every "contactless" row
-- -- the re-payment is the identity, and the assertion is the same claim
-- made twice. That is a consequence of not special-casing rows, and is
-- cheaper than a rule about which rows to exempt.

#ASSERT (`the rate on` `Citi Rewards` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` (`S5 the same purchase paid with a wallet token` `S5 dining out`))'s `earns the bonus rate` EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT (`the rate on` `Citi Rewards` `S5 the cardholder` `S5 the month` (`S5 the same purchase paid with a wallet token` `S5 a cafe`))'s `earns the bonus rate` EQUALS FALSE
